  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n We will build upon the parent solution (initial_h_values) and strive for further improvement by \n 1. Using multi-resolution optimization: starting from the parent resolution (n=620) and then exploring various coarser and finer resolutions within a time budget.\n 2. Employing Fourier series initialization as a smoother alternative to random initialization, since the problem is inherently periodic.\n 3. Increasing the maximum number of variable-frequency optimization steps in focused descent phases.\n 4. Using multiple restarts with resampling and space-filling curve seeding to escape local optima.\n 5. Incorporating warmup phases to stabilize the initial solution and ensure it meets the constraints.\n\n Mathematical specification\n We represent the step function as a Fourier series truncated at order K:\n h(x) = 0.5 + a*cos(nx) + b*sin(nx) + ... \n truncated to K terms, where a and b are Fourier coefficients at orders 1 to K.\n The domain is [0, 2] (period 2) which naturally accommodates periodic behavior observed in the overlap problem.\n\n The objective is to minimize:\n C_5 = max_{k} \u222b h(x) * (1-h(x+k)) dx\n\n Formally, the integral is approximated by:\n C = (2.0/n) * max_lag |<h_roll, 1-h>|\n where h_roll is a rolled-out version of h for the current lag.\n\n Constraints: 0 <= h(x) <= 1, and the integral over [0,2] is exactly 1.\n\n Due to boundary conditions, we numerically enforce 0<=h(x)<=1 and integral=1 via repairing steps and Bayesian optimization parameters.\n\n The search space is parameterized by the Fourier coefficients (a_j, b_j) for j=1..K, each with bounds [-0.5,0.5].\n\n Implementation procedure\n Phase 0: Repair the parent solution and ensure it meets constraints.\n\nPhase 1: Fourier initialization (multiple seeds)\n  - For each seed:\n      Initialize h using Fourier_series(n, K, seed)\n      Then repair by iterative clipping and integral adjustment\n  - Use various K (3,5,8,12) for fine-grained exploration\n  - Use the same n as parent (620)\n\nPhase 2: Multi-resolution exploration\n  - Sample from [100, 900] points (finer at low resolution, coarser at high)\n  - Use parent solution as fallback if any deterioration occurs\n  - For each resolution:\n      Sample multiple initial points (4 per resolution)\n      Use SLSQP with bar-stacking to enforce integral=1\n      Then fine-tune with focused descent\n\nPhase 3: Optimization with variable-frequency descent\n  - Structure:\n    * Warmup phase (Integral satisfaction via gradient adjustment)\n    * Core optimization:\n        for iter in range(r):\n            Sample j from top lags (lag set size increases with iteration)\n            Compute gradient descent along top k (from 1 to min(5, j))\n            Apply line search\n    * Use Bayesian optimization for maximum likelihood descent\n\nPhase 4: Adaptive search scheduling\n  - Duration-based allocation (major phases accounted 30% each):\n    1. Initialization & repair (300s)\n    2. Local refinement (300s)\n    3. Global perturbation (300s)\n  - Check viability at 500s (abort if no progress)\n\nPhase 5: Validation and repair\n  - Before return: repair final candidate\n  - Verify objective consistency by re-evaluation\n  - Use parent solution if time limit hit (fallback)\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n  - Fourier orders (K): [3,5,8,12] with allocation based on initial score improvement (higher K if improving)\n  - Resolutions: [100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900] (tested in increasing fineness)\n  - Lag depth: {l2: top 3 lags, l5: top 5 lags} triggered by convergence signals\n  - Perturbation scale: 0.01 * sqrt(Resolving) (depends on resolution adjustment)\n  - SLSQP hyperparameters: initialmaxit=2000; adaptive alpha: [10000, 100000, 1000000]\n\nComputational budget and robustness\n  - Main time sink: Variable-frequency optimization (300s recommended)\n  - We expect 0.75 improvement over parent (0.3809 -> ~0.35)\n  - Fallback: Repair version of parent solution at time-out\n  - Memory profile: Linear in resolution (n ~ 900, ~8,000 entries)\n\nValidation and return contract\n  - Precondition: n_points must be integer\n  - Postcondition: 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)*dx==1\n  - Value contract: C_5 = (2/n) * max_lag |correlation result| (clamped by feasibility)\n  - Repair mechanism: Gradient projection ensures feasible durations\n\n<diag>\nRepairing parent solution... OK\n</diag>",
